The workflow is saved and starts a call. I sent one test message ("Hello?"); the agent replied with the opening sentence (digital assistant from Freya Bank, call is recorded) and asked "Am I speaking with Ms Lee?". That is the only live test: per the instructions I ran no practice cases, so the identity, payment-promise and closing branches have not been tried.

How it is built, in `out/main-B.workflow.json`:
- **Order of steps:** the date code step you gave me runs first, unchanged. Then come the opening, the security questions, a silent account lookup, and one node for the payment talk. Every stage that can end the call has `close_call`, and a successful `close_call` moves to the end node, which says the goodbye.
- **Moving between steps:** the call moves to the security questions only when the customer clearly confirms who they are. It moves to the account lookup only when `verify_identity` says both answers passed.
- **Account lookup:** `get_account` uses your account hook unchanged. The prompt gives the model each product's own deadline and minimum amount ("floor"), with `{{date_facts}}` and your sentence telling it never to compute a date.
- **Brief rules in the prompts:** both rules the code does not cover are in the prompts in the brief's words. The date-of-birth format rule is in the security-questions node; the "one date for everything" rule is in the payment node.
- **Promise node:** it is told to offer exactly the `offer_instead` value when a result has one. It must try a failed promise once more, then note it as `service_error`. It adds no notes on any transfer.

One small deviation: I also gave the transfer tool to the opening and security-question stages, so a customer who insists on a human can be transferred before verification (brief A rule 42). Otherwise nothing differs from the date facts.

